WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.082
This query starts from patients and left joins admissions.

00:00:04.082 --> 00:00:08.232
The patient table contributes one descriptive row per patient.

00:00:08.232 --> 00:00:11.357
Admissions provides the events being counted.

00:00:11.357 --> 00:00:16.982
Filtered counts create January, February, and March admission columns.

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:20.642
The GROUP BY keeps one output row per patient.

00:00:20.642 --> 00:00:25.757
Because the join relationship is understood, the counts remain meaningful.

00:00:25.757 --> 00:00:32.397
Connect this idea to a machine-learning workflow converting wide measurements into tidy long data.

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:38.082
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:38.082 --> 00:00:45.912
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:45.912 --> 00:00:52.062
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:58.240
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
